  "assessment_summary": "This methodological guide proposes a structured approach to adjust effect sizes from RCTs (and some quasi-experiments) for use in cost-effectiveness analyses by decomposing “discounts” into internal replicability (Type M magnitude inflation and Type S sign errors) and external validity. It synthesizes literature on power, publication bias, effect-size exaggeration, and cross-context heterogeneity, and provides stepwise guidance, sanity checks using published replication/meta-analytic estimates, and a worked example. The conceptual decomposition is sound and useful, and the focus on study power, pre-registration, and mechanistic transportability is well aligned with best practice. The document is generally careful about caveats and provides actionable advice for applied analysts.\n\nImportant issues and limitations:\n\n- Quantification via multiplicative discounts: The core workflow combines several adjustments (power-related exaggeration, methodological flaws, researcher bias, and Hawthorne/special care) by multiplying “inflation factors” and inverting. This implicitly assumes independence among error sources and risks double counting correlated influences (e.g., low power and publication selection; or Hawthorne vs special care). The text gestures at these dependencies but does not provide a formal rule for dependence or caps to prevent over-correction. A decision tree or explicit dependency structure would increase reliability.\n\n- Calibration and external validation: Although the guide cites replication shrinkage and publication-bias-adjusted estimates from several fields, the recommended headline ranges (e.g., “many RCTs will need a 50–60% Type M discount”) are not calibrated specifically to the intervention domains and outcome types most common in LMIC global health/development CEAs. The worked example assigns 10–20% adjustments for specific factors without an empirical basis. A retrospective calibration exercise—comparing discounted predictions to later replications or scale-ups across a portfolio—would help refine default values and quantify uncertainty.\n\n- Power/MDE guidance and a technical error: Diagnosing underpowered studies is central here, but guidance is mixed. The document incorrectly states: “if the MDE is higher than your best estimate of the effect size, it is reasonably well-powered”; this inequality is reversed (a study is well-powered when the expected effect is larger than the MDE). The simple power plots shown (for two-sample t-tests) may mislead for cluster-randomized trials, unequal group sizes, binary outcomes, or non-normal data, which are common in this domain. Clearer warnings and links to cluster-adjusted power/MDE tools would prevent misuse. The suggestion to use the coefficient of variation as a noise heuristic can fail when means are near zero or can be negative.\n\n- Baseline priors and retrodesign: Encouraging analysts to form priors and use retrodesign to quantify Type M and Type S is a strength. However, the procedure to construct priors is under-specified and the worked example uses ad-hoc multiplicative shrinkages (e.g., reducing the mean because a share of studies were null, plus a blanket inflation correction) that do not correspond to a coherent probabilistic prior. Providing a minimal Bayesian template (e.g., normal prior on standardized effects informed by related interventions) and code would improve consistency.\n\n- Scope and transferability of evidence: Table 1 aggregates replication shrinkage across psychology, medicine, and experimental economics; the text notes heterogeneity but does not clearly delineate when each source should inform LMIC health/development RCT adjustments. For instance, Button et al.’s neuroscience exaggeration factors may not be representative for large-sample administrative outcomes, while behavior-change field studies may show larger inflations. The Coville & Vivalt development-econ estimates are closest in domain; more weight on these (and similar) sources would be appropriate.\n\n- External validity quantification: The mechanistic framework (sample differences, Hawthorne, equilibrium, special care) is appropriate and the use of Vivalt (2020) as an upper bound is reasonable. Yet translating these categories into numeric discounts remains largely judgment-based. Where feasible, anchors from the literature (e.g., NGO vs government implementation gaps; effect dependence on baseline prevalence or access measures) could be formalized into default priors with uncertainty, rather than point discounts. A clearer process for avoiding overlap between internal Hawthorne effects and external implementation changes would also help.\n\n- Type S adjustments: The guidance to restrict Type S adjustments to a minority of cases (plausible sign reversal, low power, early-stage evidence) is sensible and supported by the cited development-econ calculations. However, the estimation still depends on a prior for the true effect; without a shared library of priors by intervention family (or prediction-market-derived priors), analysts may produce highly variable estimates.\n\n- Quasi-experimental designs: The brief section correctly emphasizes design assumptions (IV, RD) and cites within-study comparisons showing limited systematic bias, but there is no concrete checklist or quantification rubric akin to the RCT section. Given frequent reliance on quasi-experiments in CEAs, a more developed protocol would be valuable.\n\n- Reproducibility and open resources: The guidance references external tools (R packages, JASP), a “discounts library,” and other links not embedded or archived in the PDF. No code, workbook, or templates are provided for the end-to-end workflow (e.g., power/MDE for clusters, retrodesign with alternative priors, dependency-aware discount aggregation). This constrains reproducibility and comparability across analysts.\n\n- Communication and internal consistency: Overall, the writing is clear and well structured, and the internal vs external validity split is well motivated. There are minor typographical issues and the single substantive MDE inequality error noted above. Some statements are overly categorical (e.g., “all RCTs will need a Type M adjustment”) where “most RCTs, especially early/underpowered ones” would be safer. The worked example is helpful but relies on unreferenced percentages; clearer justification or ranges would help users calibrate.\n\nIn sum, this is a thoughtful, practice-oriented synthesis likely to improve CEA robustness by counteracting effect-size inflation and improving attention to transportability. Its main weaknesses are reliance on cross-field averages without domain-specific calibration, a few technical misstatements and simplifications in power diagnostics, subjective and potentially dependent adjustments aggregated multiplicatively, and limited open-science assets. The “work in progress” framing is appropriate; adding validated default priors and ranges, code/templates, a dependency-aware aggregation scheme, and retrospective validation against realized replications or scale-ups would substantially strengthen both credibility and usability.",
